msgid "[4.7.2, \"Buffers\"][SYCL-2020-4.7.2]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13519
msgid "[4.7.6, \"Accessors\"][SYCL-2020-4.7.6]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13520
msgid "[4.7.7, \"Address space classes\"][SYCL-2020-4.7.7]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13521
msgid "[F.7, \"sycl_khr_static_addrspace_cast\"][SYCL-2020-F.7]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13522
msgid "[F.8, \"sycl_khr_dynamic_addrspace_cast\"][SYCL-2020-F.8]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13525
msgid ""
"The SYCL address space attributes listed below correspond to the five "
"address spaces described by [SYCL 2020 section 3.8.2, \"SYCL device memory "
"model\"][SYCL-2020-3.8.2] and [SYCL 2020 section 4.7.7, \"Address space "
"classes\"][SYCL-2020-4.7.7]."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13530
msgid "SYCL address space attributes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13533
msgid "Address space attribute"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13534
msgid "SYCL address space"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13535
msgid "Description"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13536
msgid "`[[clang::sycl_global]]`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13537 ../../../AttributeReference.md:15692
msgid "global"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13538
msgid "A memory region accessible by all work-items executing on a device."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13539
msgid "`[[clang::sycl_local]]`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13540
msgid "local"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13541
msgid "A memory region accessible by all work-items of a single work-group."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13542
msgid "`[[clang::sycl_private]]`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13543
msgid "private"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13544
msgid "A memory region that is private to a single work-item."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13545
msgid "`[[clang::sycl_generic]]`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13546
msgid "generic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13547
msgid ""
"A virtual memory region from which the global, local, and private memory "
"regions may all be accessed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13549
msgid "`[[clang::sycl_constant]]`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13550 ../../../AttributeReference.md:15536
msgid "constant"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13551
msgid ""
"(*deprecated*) A memory region that holds constant data for an executing "
"kernel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13555
msgid ""
"The SYCL address space attributes are type attributes that may be applied to "
"non-function non-reference types to specify an address space qualified type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13558
msgid ""
"A type with a SYCL address space qualifier is a distinct type from the "
"otherwise unattributed type. For example, `int *` and `int "
"[[clang::sycl_global]]*` designate distinct pointer types which participate "
"in overload resolution and template specialization."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13563
msgid ""
"The top-level type of a variable declaration cannot have a SYCL address "
"space qualifier. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13571
msgid ""
"Conversions between SYCL address space attributed types are permitted as "
"follows."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13574
msgid ""
"Types attributed with the global, local, or private address space attributes "
"are implicitly convertible to matching types with the generic address space "
"attribute."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13578
msgid ""
"The mapping of SYCL address spaces to physical address spaces is target "
"dependent."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13581
msgid ""
"For OpenCL device targets, the SYCL address space attributes are aligned "
"with the [OpenCL address space attributes](#opencl-address-spaces) such "
"that, e.g., `int [[clang::sycl_global]]*` and `int "
"[[clang::opencl_global]]*` specify distinct types both of which map to the "
"same underlying address space. Corresponding SYCL and OpenCL address space "
"attributed types are implicitly convertible; other conversions are permitted "
"as described above; e.g., `int [[clang::sycl_global]]*` is implicitly "
"convertible to `int [[clang::opencl_generic]]*`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13598
msgid "__ptr32"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13615
msgid "`__ptr32`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13621
msgid ""
"The `__ptr32` qualifier represents a native pointer on a 32-bit system. On a "
"64-bit system, a pointer with `__ptr32` is extended to a 64-bit pointer. The "
"`__sptr` and `__uptr` qualifiers can be used to specify whether the pointer "
"is sign extended or zero extended. This qualifier is enabled under `-fms-"
"extensions`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13628
msgid "__ptr64"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13645
msgid "`__ptr64`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13651
msgid ""
"The `__ptr64` qualifier represents a native pointer on a 64-bit system. On a "
"32-bit system, a `__ptr64` pointer is truncated to a 32-bit pointer. This "
"qualifier is enabled under `-fms-extensions`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13656
msgid "__sptr"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13673
msgid "`__sptr`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13679
msgid ""
"The `__sptr` qualifier specifies that a 32-bit pointer should be sign "
"extended when converted to a 64-bit pointer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13683
msgid "__uptr"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13700
msgid "`__uptr`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13706
msgid ""
"The `__uptr` qualifier specifies that a 32-bit pointer should be zero "
"extended when converted to a 64-bit pointer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13712
msgid "address_space"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13725
msgid "`address_space`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13726 ../../../AttributeReference.md:13727
msgid "`clang::address_space`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13736
msgid ""
"This attribute is mainly intended to be used by target headers provided by "
"the toolchain. End users should prefer the documented, named address space "
"annotations for their platform, such as the [OpenCL address spaces](#opencl-"
"address-spaces), `__global__`, `__local__`, or something else."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13743
msgid ""
"The `address_space` attribute functions as a type qualifier that allows the "
"programmer to specify the address space for a pointer or reference type. "
"Qualified pointer types are considered distinct types for the purposes of "
"overload resolution. The attribute takes a single, non-negative integer "
"constant expression identifying the address space. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13755
msgid ""
"Only one address space qualifier may be applied to a given pointer or "
"reference type. Where address spaces are allowed (e.g., variables, "
"parameters, return types) and what values are valid depends on the target "
"and language mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13759
msgid ""
"The meaning of each value is defined by the target; multiple address spaces "
"are used in environments such as OpenCL, CUDA, HIP, and other GPU "
"programming models to distinguish global, local, constant, and private "
"memory. See for example the address spaces defined in the [NVPTX User Guide]"
"[nvptx user guide] and the [AMDGPU User Guide][amdgpu user guide]."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13765
msgid ""
"Address spaces may partially overlap or be entirely distinct. The compiler "
"may reject attempts to convert between distinct, incompatible address "
"spaces. Pointer width may vary between different address spaces, so some "
"explicit casts may truncate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13770
msgid ""
"For more information, refer to [ISO TR18037][iso tr18037], which covers "
"embedded C language extensions. Section 5 covers named address spaces."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13778
msgid "align_value"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13791
msgid "`align_value`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13801
msgid ""
"The align_value attribute can be added to the typedef of a pointer type or "
"the declaration of a variable of pointer or reference type. It specifies "
"that the pointer will point to, or the reference will bind to, only objects "
"with at least the provided alignment. This alignment value must be some "
"positive power of 2."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13813
msgid ""
"If the pointer value does not have the specified alignment at runtime, the "
"behavior of the program is undefined."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13817
msgid "annotate_type"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13831 ../../../AttributeReference.md:13832
msgid "`clang::annotate_type`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13840
msgid ""
"This attribute is used to add annotations to types, typically for use by "
"static analysis tools that are not integrated into the core Clang compiler "
"(e.g., Clang-Tidy checks or out-of-tree Clang-based tools). It is a "
"counterpart to the `annotate` attribute, which serves the same purpose, but "
"for declarations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13856
msgid ""
"The attribute does not have any effect on the semantics of the type system, "
"neither type checking rules, nor runtime semantics. In particular:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13859
msgid ""
"`std::is_same<T, T [[clang::annotate_type(\"foo\")]]>` is true for all types "
"`T`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13861
msgid ""
"It is not permissible for overloaded functions or template specializations "
"to differ merely by an `annotate_type` attribute."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13863
msgid ""
"The presence of an `annotate_type` attribute will not affect name mangling."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13867
msgid "arm_sve_vector_bits"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13880
msgid "`arm_sve_vector_bits`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13890
msgid ""
"The `arm_sve_vector_bits(N)` attribute is defined by the Arm C Language "
"Extensions (ACLE) for SVE. It is used to define fixed-length (VLST) variants "
"of sizeless types (VLAT)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13904
msgid ""
"Creates a type `fixed_svint32_t` that is a fixed-length variant of "
"`svint32_t` that contains exactly 512-bits. Unlike `svint32_t`, this type "
"can be used in globals, structs, unions, and arrays, all of which are "
"unsupported for sizeless types."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13909
msgid ""
"The attribute can be attached to a single SVE vector (such as `svint32_t`) "
"or to the SVE predicate type `svbool_t`, this excludes tuple types such as "
"`svint32x4_t`. The behavior of the attribute is undefined unless "
"`N==__ARM_FEATURE_SVE_BITS`, the implementation defined feature macro that "
"is enabled under the `-msve-vector-bits` flag."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13915
msgid ""
"For more information See [Arm C Language Extensions for SVE](https://"
"developer.arm.com/documentation/100987/latest) for more information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13918
msgid "bpf_fastcall"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13931
msgid "`bpf_fastcall`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13932 ../../../AttributeReference.md:13933
msgid "`clang::bpf_fastcall`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13941
msgid ""
"Functions annotated with this attribute are likely to be inlined by BPF JIT. "
"It is assumed that inlined implementation uses less caller saved registers, "
"than a regular function. Specifically, the following registers are likely to "
"be preserved:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13945
msgid "`R0` if function return value is `void`;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13946
msgid "`R2-R5` if function takes 1 argument;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13947
msgid "`R3-R5` if function takes 2 arguments;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13948
msgid "`R4-R5` if function takes 3 arguments;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13949
msgid "`R5` if function takes 4 arguments;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13951
msgid ""
"For such functions Clang generates code pattern that allows BPF JIT to "
"recognize and remove unnecessary spills and fills of the preserved registers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13956
msgid "btf_type_tag"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13969
msgid "`btf_type_tag`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13970 ../../../AttributeReference.md:13971
msgid "`clang::btf_type_tag`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13979
msgid ""
"Clang supports the `__attribute__((btf_type_tag(\"ARGUMENT\")))` attribute "
"for all targets. It only has effect when `-g` is specified on the command "
"line."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13982
msgid ""
"The attribute can be applied to a pointer type, in which case the tag is "
"associated with the pointee type, e.g.:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13989
msgid ""
"It can also be applied to the underlying type of a typedef, in which case "
"the tag follows the typedef down to its base type, e.g.:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:13996
msgid "The following is the corresponding btf:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14008
msgid ""
"The attribute is currently silently ignored in any other position (note: "
"this scenario may be diagnosed in the future)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14011
msgid ""
"The `ARGUMENT` string will be preserved in IR and emitted to DWARF for the "
"types used in variable declarations, function declarations, or typedef "
"declarations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14015
msgid ""
"For BPF targets, the `ARGUMENT` string will also be emitted to .BTF ELF "
"section."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14019
msgid "cfi_unchecked_callee"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14032
msgid "`cfi_unchecked_callee`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14033 ../../../AttributeReference.md:14034
msgid "`clang::cfi_unchecked_callee`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14042
msgid ""
"`cfi_unchecked_callee` is a function type attribute which prevents the "
"compiler from instrumenting {doc}`Control Flow Integrity "
"<ControlFlowIntegrity>` checks on indirect function calls. This also "
"includes control flow checks added by `-fsanitize=function`; see {ref}"
"`Available checks <ubsan-checks>`. Specifically, the attribute has the "
"following semantics:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14049
msgid ""
"Indirect calls to a function type with this attribute will not be "
"instrumented with CFI. That is, the indirect call will not be checked. Note "
"that this only changes the behavior for indirect calls on pointers to "
"function types having this attribute. It does not prevent all indirect "
"function calls for a given type from being checked."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14053
msgid ""
"All direct references to a function whose type has this attribute will "
"always reference the function definition rather than an entry in the CFI "
"jump table."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14055
msgid ""
"When a pointer to a function with this attribute is implicitly cast to a "
"pointer to a function without this attribute, the compiler will give a "
"warning saying this attribute is discarded. This warning can be silenced "
"with an explicit cast. Note an explicit cast just disables the warning, so "
"direct references to a function with a `cfi_unchecked_callee` attribute will "
"still reference the function definition rather than the CFI jump table."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14082
msgid ""
"This attribute can only be applied on functions or member functions. This "
"attribute can be a good alternative to `no_sanitize(\"cfi\")` if you only "
"want to disable innstrumentation for specific indirect calls rather than "
"applying `no_sanitize(\"cfi\")` on the whole function containing indirect "
"call. Note that `cfi_unchecked_attribute` is a type attribute doesn't "
"disable CFI instrumentation on a function body."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14089
msgid "clang_arm_mve_strict_polymorphism"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14102
msgid "`__clang_arm_mve_strict_polymorphism`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14103 ../../../AttributeReference.md:14104
msgid "`clang::__clang_arm_mve_strict_polymorphism`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14112
msgid ""
"This attribute is used in the implementation of the ACLE intrinsics for the "
"Arm MVE instruction set. It is used to define the vector types used by the "
"MVE intrinsics."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14116
msgid ""
"Its effect is to modify the behavior of a vector type with respect to "
"function overloading. If a candidate function for overload resolution has a "
"parameter type with this attribute, then the selection of that candidate "
"function will be disallowed if the actual argument can only be converted via "
"a lax vector conversion. The aim is to prevent spurious ambiguity in ARM MVE "
"polymorphic intrinsics."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14137
msgid ""
"However, this attribute does not prohibit lax vector conversions in contexts "
"other than overloading."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14149
msgid "cmse_nonsecure_call"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14162
msgid "`cmse_nonsecure_call`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14172
msgid ""
"This attribute declares a non-secure function type. When compiling for "
"secure state, a call to such a function would switch from secure to non-"
"secure state. All non-secure function calls must happen only through a "
"function pointer, and a non-secure function type should only be used as a "
"base type of a pointer. See [ARMv8-M Security Extensions: Requirements on "
"Development Tools - Engineering Specification Documentation](https://"
"developer.arm.com/docs/ecm0359818/latest/) for more information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14180
msgid "contained_type"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14194
msgid "`hlsl::contained_type`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14203
msgid ""
"The `hlsl::contained_type` attribute specifies the type of the HLSL resource "
"represented by a member variable of type `__hlsl_resource_t`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14206 ../../../AttributeReference.md:14298
#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14389 ../../../AttributeReference.md:14420
#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14451 ../../../AttributeReference.md:14482
#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14690 ../../../AttributeReference.md:14722
msgid ""
"This attribute is only valid for resource handles, and is an implementation "
"detail of clang's HLSL implementation. For more information see {doc}`HLSL "
"Resource Types <HLSL/ResourceTypes>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14211
msgid "device_builtin_surface_type"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14224
msgid "`device_builtin_surface_type`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14227
msgid "`__device_builtin_surface_type__`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14234
msgid ""
"The `device_builtin_surface_type` attribute can be applied to a class "
"template when declaring the surface reference. A surface reference variable "
"could be accessed on the host side and, on the device side, might be "
"translated into an internal surface object, which is established through "
"surface bind and unbind runtime APIs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14241
msgid "device_builtin_texture_type"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14254
msgid "`device_builtin_texture_type`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14257
msgid "`__device_builtin_texture_type__`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14264
msgid ""
"The `device_builtin_texture_type` attribute can be applied to a class "
"template when declaring the texture reference. A texture reference variable "
"could be accessed on the host side and, on the device side, might be "
"translated into an internal texture object, which is established through "
"texture bind and unbind runtime APIs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14271
msgid "dimension"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14285
msgid "`hlsl::dimension`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14294
msgid ""
"The `hlsl::dimension` attribute specifies the dimensions of the HLSL "
"resource represented by a member variable of type `__hlsl_resource_t`, "
"declaring the resource to have Unknown, 1D, 2D, 3D, or Cube dimension."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14303
msgid "enforce_read_only_placement"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14316
msgid "`enforce_read_only_placement`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14317 ../../../AttributeReference.md:14318
msgid "`clang::enforce_read_only_placement`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14326
msgid "This attribute is attached to a structure, class or union declaration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14328
msgid ""
"When attached to a record declaration/definition, it checks if all instances "
"of this type can be placed in the read-only data segment of the program. If "
"it finds an instance that can not be placed in a read-only segment, the "
"compiler emits a warning at the source location where the type was used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14334
msgid "`struct __attribute__((enforce_read_only_placement)) Foo;`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14335
msgid "`struct __attribute__((enforce_read_only_placement)) Bar { ... };`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14337
msgid ""
"Both `Foo` and `Bar` types have the `enforce_read_only_placement` attribute."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14339
msgid ""
"The goal of introducing this attribute is to assist developers with writing "
"secure code. A `const`-qualified global is generally placed in the read-only "
"section of the memory that has additional run time protection from malicious "
"writes. By attaching this attribute to a declaration, the developer can "
"express the intent to place all instances of the annotated type in the read-"
"only program memory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14345
msgid ""
"Note 1: The attribute doesn't guarantee that the object will be placed in "
"the read-only data segment as it does not instruct the compiler to ensure "
"such a placement. It emits a warning if something in the code can be proven "
"to prevent an instance from being placed in the read-only data segment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14350
msgid ""
"Note 2: Currently, clang only checks if all global declarations of a given "
"type `T` are `const`-qualified. The following conditions would also prevent "
"the data to be put into read only segment, but the corresponding warnings "
"are not yet implemented."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14354
msgid "An instance of type `T` is allocated on the heap/stack."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14355
msgid "Type `T` defines/inherits a mutable field."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14356
msgid ""
"Type `T` defines/inherits non-constexpr constructor(s) for initialization."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14357
msgid ""
"A field of type `T` is defined by type `Q`, which does not bear the "
"`enforce_read_only_placement` attribute."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14359
msgid ""
"A type `Q` inherits from type `T` and it does not have the "
"`enforce_read_only_placement` attribute."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14363
msgid "is_array"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14377
msgid "`hlsl::is_array`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14386
msgid ""
"The `hlsl::is_array` attribute specifies that the HLSL resource represented "
"by a member variable of type `__hlsl_resource_t` has array dimensions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14394
msgid "is_counter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14408
msgid "`hlsl::is_counter`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14417
msgid ""
"The `hlsl::is_counter` attribute specifies that the HLSL resource "
"represented by a member variable of type `__hlsl_resource_t` is a counter "
"buffer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14425
msgid "is_ms"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14439
msgid "`hlsl::is_ms`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14448
msgid ""
"The `hlsl::is_array` attribute specifies that the HLSL resource represented "
"by a member variable of type `__hlsl_resource_t` is multisampled."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14456
msgid "is_rov"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14470
msgid "`hlsl::is_rov`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14479
msgid ""
"The `hlsl::is_rov` attribute specifies that the HLSL resource represented by "
"a member variable of type `__hlsl_resource_t` is a rasterizer ordered view."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14487
msgid "noderef"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14500
msgid "`noderef`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14501 ../../../AttributeReference.md:14502
msgid "`clang::noderef`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14510
msgid ""
"The `noderef` attribute causes clang to diagnose dereferences of annotated "
"pointer types. This is ideally used with pointers that point to special "
"memory which cannot be read from or written to, but allowing for the pointer "
"to be used in pointer arithmetic. The following are examples of valid "
"expressions where dereferences are diagnosed:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14533
msgid ""
"Not all dereferences may diagnose a warning if the value directed by the "
"pointer may not be accessed. The following are examples of valid expressions "
"where may not be diagnosed:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14550
msgid ""
"`noderef` is currently only supported for pointers and arrays and not usable "
"for references or Objective-C object pointers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14563
msgid "objc_class_stub"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14576
msgid "`objc_class_stub`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14577 ../../../AttributeReference.md:14578
msgid "`clang::objc_class_stub`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14586
msgid ""
"This attribute specifies that the Objective-C class to which it applies is "
"instantiated at runtime."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14589
msgid ""
"Unlike `__attribute__((objc_runtime_visible))`, a class having this "
"attribute still has a \"class stub\" that is visible to the linker. This "
"allows categories to be defined. Static message sends with the class as a "
"receiver use a special access pattern to ensure the class is lazily "
"instantiated from the class stub."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14594
msgid ""
"Classes annotated with this attribute cannot be subclassed and cannot have "
"implementations defined for them. This attribute is intended for use in "
"Swift-generated headers for classes defined in Swift."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14598
msgid "Adding or removing this attribute to a class is an ABI-breaking change."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14601
msgid "overflow_behavior"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14614
msgid "`overflow_behavior`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14615 ../../../AttributeReference.md:14616
msgid "`clang::overflow_behavior`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14624
msgid ""
"The `overflow_behavior` attribute provides fine-grained, type-level control "
"over how arithmetic operations on an integer type behave on overflow. It may "
"be applied to a `typedef`, to a variable or data member, or to an integer "
"type directly, and accepts one of two behaviors as its argument:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14629
msgid ""
"`wrap`: arithmetic on the attributed type wraps on overflow, using two's "
"complement semantics. This is equivalent to `-fwrapv` but scoped to the "
"attributed type, and works for both signed and unsigned types. UBSan's "
"`signed-integer-overflow`, `unsigned-integer-overflow`, `implicit-signed-"
"integer-truncation`, and `implicit-unsigned-integer-truncation` checks are "
"suppressed for the type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14635
msgid ""
"`trap`: arithmetic on the attributed type is checked for overflow, enabling "
"overflow checks for the type even when `-fwrapv` is in effect globally."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14651
msgid ""
"The keyword spellings `__ob_wrap` and `__ob_trap` are equivalent to "
"`overflow_behavior(wrap)` and `overflow_behavior(trap)` respectively."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14654
msgid ""
"The attribute wholly overrides global flags (`-ftrapv`, `-fwrapv`, "
"sanitizers, and Sanitizer Special Case Lists) for the attributed type. It "
"can only be applied to integer types."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14658
msgid ""
"This feature is experimental and must be enabled with the `-cc1` option `-"
"fexperimental-overflow-behavior-types`. For full details on promotion and "
"conversion rules, pointer semantics, diagnostics, and interaction with "
"sanitizers, see {doc}`OverflowBehaviorTypes`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14664
msgid "raw_buffer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14678
msgid "`hlsl::raw_buffer`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14687
msgid ""
"The `hlsl::raw_buffer` attribute specifies that the HLSL resource "
"represented by a member variable of type `__hlsl_resource_t` has raw buffer "
"semantics."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14695
msgid "resource_class"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14709
msgid "`hlsl::resource_class`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14718
msgid ""
"The `hlsl::resource_class` attribute specifies the resource class of the "
"HLSL resource represented by a member variable of type `__hlsl_resource_t`, "
"declaring it to be an SRV, UAV, CBuffer, or Sampler resource."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14727
msgid "riscv_rvv_vector_bits"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14740
msgid "`riscv_rvv_vector_bits`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14750
msgid ""
"On RISC-V targets, the `riscv_rvv_vector_bits(N)` attribute is used to "
"define fixed-length variants of sizeless types."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14763
msgid ""
"Creates a type `fixed_vint8m1_t` that is a fixed-length variant of "
"`vint8m1_t` that contains exactly 512 bits. Unlike `vint8m1_t`, this type "
"can be used in globals, structs, unions, and arrays, all of which are "
"unsupported for sizeless types."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14768
msgid ""
"The attribute can be attached to a single RVV vector (such as `vint8m1_t`). "
"The attribute will be rejected unless `N==(__riscv_v_fixed_vlen*LMUL)`, the "
"implementation defined feature macro that is enabled under the `-mrvv-vector-"
"bits` flag. `__riscv_v_fixed_vlen` can only be a power of 2 between 64 and "
"65536."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14774
msgid ""
"For types where LMUL!=1, `__riscv_v_fixed_vlen` needs to be scaled by the "
"LMUL of the type before passing to the attribute."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14777
msgid ""
"For `vbool*_t` types, `__riscv_v_fixed_vlen` needs to be divided by the "
"number from the type name. For example, `vbool8_t` needs to use "
"`__riscv_v_fixed_vlen` / 8. If the resulting value is not a multiple of 8, "
"the type is not supported for that value of `__riscv_v_fixed_vlen`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14783
msgid "type_visibility"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14796
msgid "`type_visibility`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14797 ../../../AttributeReference.md:14798
msgid "`clang::type_visibility`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14806
msgid ""
"The `type_visibility` attribute allows the visibility of a type and its "
"vague linkage objects (vtable, typeinfo, typeinfo name) to be controlled "
"separately from the visibility of functions and data members of the type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14810
msgid ""
"For example, this can be used to give default visibility to the typeinfo and "
"the vtable of a type while still keeping hidden visibility on its member "
"functions and static data members."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14814
msgid "This attribute can only be applied to types and namespaces."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14816
msgid ""
"If both `visibility` and `type_visibility` are applied to a type or a "
"namespace, the visibility specified with the `type_visibility` attribute "
"overrides the visibility provided with the regular `visibility` attribute."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14821
msgid "warn_unused"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14834
msgid "`warn_unused`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14835 ../../../AttributeReference.md:14836
msgid "`gnu::warn_unused`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14844
msgid ""
"The `warn_unused` attribute can be placed on the declaration of a structure "
"or union type. When the `-Wunused-variable` diagnostic is enabled, local "
"variables of types which have a non-trivial constructor or destructor are "
"considered \"used\" by virtue of the constructor or destructor invocations "
"involved. Those constructor or destructor invocations are not considered a "
"use if the type is declared with the `warn_unused` attribute. The variable "
"is considered used if it is named outside of its declaration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14849
msgid ""
"This attribute is available in both C and C++ language modes but is "
"primarily useful in C++ for classes which have a non-trivial constructor or "
"destructor but act as a value type rather than an RAII type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14874
msgid "Type Safety Checking"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14876
msgid ""
"Clang supports additional attributes to enable checking type safety "
"properties that can't be enforced by the C type system. To see warnings "
"produced by these checks, ensure that -Wtype-safety is enabled. Use cases "
"include:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14880
msgid ""
"MPI library implementations, where these attributes enable checking that the "
"buffer type matches the passed `MPI_Datatype`;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14882
msgid "for HDF5 library there is a similar use case to MPI;"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14883
msgid ""
"checking types of variadic functions' arguments for functions like `fcntl()` "
"and `ioctl()`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14886
msgid ""
"You can detect support for these attributes with `__has_attribute()`. For "
"example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14907
msgid "argument_with_type_tag"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14920 ../../../AttributeReference.md:14973
msgid "`argument_with_type_tag` <br/> `pointer_with_type_tag`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14921 ../../../AttributeReference.md:14922
#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14974 ../../../AttributeReference.md:14975
msgid "`clang::argument_with_type_tag` <br/> `clang::pointer_with_type_tag`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14930
msgid ""
"Use `__attribute__((argument_with_type_tag(arg_kind, arg_idx, "
"type_tag_idx)))` on a function declaration to specify that the function "
"accepts a type tag that determines the type of some other argument."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14934
msgid ""
"This attribute is primarily useful for checking arguments of variadic "
"functions (`pointer_with_type_tag` can be used in most non-variadic cases)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14937 ../../../AttributeReference.md:14987
#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:15040
msgid "In the attribute prototype above:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14938
msgid ""
"`arg_kind` is an identifier that should be used when annotating all "
"applicable type tags."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14940
msgid ""
"`arg_idx` provides the position of a function argument. The expected type of "
"this function argument will be determined by the function argument specified "
"by `type_tag_idx`. In the code example below, \"3\" means that the type of "
"the function's third argument will be determined by `type_tag_idx`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14944
msgid ""
"`type_tag_idx` provides the position of a function argument. This function "
"argument will be a type tag. The type tag will determine the expected type "
"of the argument specified by `arg_idx`. In the code example below, \"2\" "
"means that the type tag associated with the function's second argument "
"should agree with the type of the argument specified by `arg_idx`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14960
msgid "pointer_with_type_tag"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14983
msgid ""
"Use `__attribute__((pointer_with_type_tag(ptr_kind, ptr_idx, "
"type_tag_idx)))` on a function declaration to specify that the function "
"accepts a type tag that determines the pointee type of some other pointer "
"argument."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14988
msgid ""
"`ptr_kind` is an identifier that should be used when annotating all "
"applicable type tags."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14990
msgid ""
"`ptr_idx` provides the position of a function argument; this function "
"argument will have a pointer type. The expected pointee type of this pointer "
"type will be determined by the function argument specified by "
"`type_tag_idx`. In the code example below, \"1\" means that the pointee type "
"of the function's first argument will be determined by `type_tag_idx`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:14995
msgid ""
"`type_tag_idx` provides the position of a function argument; this function "
"argument will be a type tag. The type tag will determine the expected "
"pointee type of the pointer argument specified by `ptr_idx`. In the code "
"example below, \"3\" means that the type tag associated with the function's "
"third argument should agree with the pointee type of the pointer argument "
"specified by `ptr_idx`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:15013
msgid "type_tag_for_datatype"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:15026
msgid "`type_tag_for_datatype`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:15027 ../../../AttributeReference.md:15028
msgid "`clang::type_tag_for_datatype`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:15036
msgid ""
"When declaring a variable, use `__attribute__((type_tag_for_datatype(kind, "
"type)))` to create a type tag that is tied to the `type` argument given to "
"the attribute."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:15041
msgid ""
"`kind` is an identifier that should be used when annotating all applicable "
"type tags."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:15043
msgid "`type` indicates the name of the type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:15045
msgid "Clang supports annotating type tags of two forms."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:15047
msgid ""
"**Type tag that is a reference to a declared identifier.** Use "
"`__attribute__((type_tag_for_datatype(kind, type)))` when declaring that "
"identifier:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:15059
msgid ""
"**Type tag that is an integral literal.** Declare a `static const` variable "
"with an initializer value and attach "
"`__attribute__((type_tag_for_datatype(kind, type)))` on that declaration:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:15071
msgid ""
"The `type_tag_for_datatype` attribute also accepts an optional third "
"argument that determines how the type of the function argument specified by "
"either `arg_idx` or `ptr_idx` is compared against the type associated with "
"the type tag. (Recall that for the `argument_with_type_tag` attribute, the "
"type of the function argument specified by `arg_idx` is compared against the "
"type associated with the type tag. Also recall that for the "
"`pointer_with_type_tag` attribute, the pointee type of the function argument "
"specified by `ptr_idx` is compared against the type associated with the type "
"tag.) There are two supported values for this optional third argument:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:15081
msgid ""
"`layout_compatible` will cause types to be compared according to layout-"
"compatibility rules (In C++11 [class.mem] p 17, 18, see the layout-"
"compatibility rules for two standard-layout struct types and for two "
"standard-layout union types). This is useful when creating a type tag "
"associated with a struct or union type. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:15116
msgid ""
"`must_be_null` specifies that the function argument specified by either "
"`arg_idx` (for the `argument_with_type_tag` attribute) or `ptr_idx` (for the "
"`pointer_with_type_tag` attribute) should be a null pointer constant. The "
"second argument to the `type_tag_for_datatype` attribute is ignored. For "
"example:"
msgstr ""

