#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17282
msgid "HLSL Interpolation Modifiers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17299
msgid ""
"`nointerpolation` <br/> `linear` <br/> `centroid` <br/> `noperspective` <br/"
"> `sample` <br/> `center`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17305
msgid ""
"The HLSL keywords `nointerpolation`, `linear`, `centroid`, `noperspective`, "
"`sample`, and `center` control interpolation of pixel shader inputs and "
"vertex shader outputs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17309
msgid ""
"When applied to an aggregate type, the modifier propagates recursively to "
"every scalar or vector of the type. A modifier on an inner field overrides "
"one inherited from an enclosing declaration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17313
msgid ""
"`nointerpolation` cannot be combined with another interpolation modifier. "
"For pixel shader inputs and vertex shader outputs, it cannot be used on "
"`SV_Position`. Integer, boolean, and 64-bit floating-point components only "
"support `nointerpolation`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17318
msgid ""
"Unqualified pixel shader inputs and vertex shader outputs default to "
"`linear` for floating-point components of at most 32 bits and "
"`nointerpolation` otherwise. In DirectX signatures, `SV_Position` uses the "
"corresponding `noperspective` mode. Vertex shader inputs, pixel shader "
"outputs, and signatures in other shader stages have no interpolation mode so "
"their interpolation modifiers are ignored."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17324
msgid ""
"For SPIR-V targets, `nointerpolation`, `noperspective`, `centroid`, and "
"`sample` emit `Flat`, `NoPerspective`, `Centroid`, and `Sample` decorations, "
"respectively. `linear` and `center` use the SPIR-V defaults and require no "
"decoration. Sample-qualified interfaces also require the `SampleRateShading` "
"capability. For `SV_Barycentrics` inputs, `noperspective` selects the "
"`BaryCoordNoPerspKHR` built-in instead of adding a `NoPerspective` "
"decoration; `centroid` and `sample` still decorate the selected built-in."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17333
msgid "HLSL Parameter Modifiers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17350
msgid "`in` <br/> `inout` <br/> `out`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17356
msgid ""
"HLSL function parameters are passed by value. Parameter declarations support "
"three qualifiers to denote parameter passing behavior. The three qualifiers "
"are `in`, `out` and `inout`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17360
msgid ""
"Parameters annotated with `in` or with no annotation are passed by value "
"from the caller to the callee."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17363
msgid ""
"Parameters annotated with `out` are written to the argument after the callee "
"returns (Note: arguments values passed into `out` parameters *are not* "
"copied into the callee)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17367
msgid ""
"Parameters annotated with `inout` are copied into the callee via a "
"temporary, and copied back to the argument after the callee returns."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17371
msgid "__ptrauth"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17388
msgid "`__ptrauth`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17394
msgid ""
"The `__ptrauth` qualifier allows the programmer to directly control how "
"pointers are signed when they are stored in a particular variable. This can "
"be used to strengthen the default protections of pointer authentication and "
"make it more difficult for an attacker to escalate an ability to alter "
"memory into full control of a process."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17407
msgid ""
"The first argument to `__ptrauth` is the name of the signing key. Valid key "
"names for the target are defined in `<ptrauth.h>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17410
msgid ""
"The second argument to `__ptrauth` is a flag (0 or 1) specifying whether the "
"object should use address discrimination."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17413
msgid ""
"The third argument to `__ptrauth` is a 16-bit non-negative integer which "
"allows additional discrimination between objects."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17417
msgid "always_destroy"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17430
msgid "`always_destroy`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17431
msgid "`clang::always_destroy`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17440
msgid ""
"The `always_destroy` attribute specifies that a variable with static or "
"thread storage duration should have its exit-time destructor run. This "
"attribute is the default unless clang was invoked with `-fno-c++-static-"
"destructors`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17444
msgid ""
"If a variable is explicitly declared with this attribute, Clang will silence "
"otherwise applicable `-Wexit-time-destructors` warnings."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17448
msgid "binding"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17462
msgid "`vk::binding`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17471
msgid ""
"The `[[vk::binding]]` attribute allows you to explicitly specify the "
"descriptor set and binding for a resource when targeting SPIR-V. This is "
"particularly useful when you need different bindings for SPIR-V and DXIL, as "
"the `register` attribute can be used for DXIL-specific bindings."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17476
msgid ""
"The attribute takes two integer arguments: the binding and the descriptor "
"set. The descriptor set is optional and defaults to 0 if not provided."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17493
msgid "called_once"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17506
msgid "`called_once`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17507 ../../../AttributeReference.md:17508
msgid "`clang::called_once`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17516
msgid ""
"The `called_once` attribute specifies that the annotated function or method "
"parameter is invoked exactly once on all execution paths. It only applies to "
"parameters with function-like types, i.e. function pointers or blocks. This "
"concept is particularly useful for asynchronous programs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17521
msgid ""
"Clang implements a check for `called_once` parameters, `-Wcalled-once-"
"parameter`. It is on by default and finds the following violations:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17525
msgid "Parameter is not called at all."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17526
msgid "Parameter is called more than once."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17527
msgid "Parameter is not called on one of the execution paths."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17529
msgid ""
"In the latter case, Clang pinpoints the path where parameter is not invoked "
"by showing the control-flow statement where the path diverges."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17558
msgid ""
"This attribute is useful for API developers who want to double-check if they "
"implemented their method correctly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17562
msgid "clang::code_align"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17575
msgid "`code_align`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17576 ../../../AttributeReference.md:17577
msgid "`clang::code_align`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17585
msgid ""
"The `clang::code_align(N)` attribute applies to a loop and specifies the "
"byte alignment for a loop. The attribute accepts a positive integer constant "
"initialization expression indicating the number of bytes for the minimum "
"alignment boundary. Its value must be a power of 2, between 1 and 4096 "
"(inclusive)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17620
msgid "cleanup"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17633
msgid "`cleanup`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17634 ../../../AttributeReference.md:17635
msgid "`gnu::cleanup`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17643
msgid ""
"This attribute allows a function to be run when a local variable goes out of "
"scope. The attribute takes the identifier of a function with a parameter "
"type that is a pointer to the type with the attribute."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17658
msgid ""
"The above example will result in a call to `bar` being passed the address of "
"`y` when `y` goes out of scope, then a call to `foo` being passed the "
"address of `x` when `x` goes out of scope. If two or more variables share "
"the same scope, their `cleanup` callbacks are invoked in the reverse order "
"the variables were declared in. It is not possible to check the return value "
"(if any) of these `cleanup` callback functions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17666
msgid "dllexport"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17679 ../../../AttributeReference.md:17682
msgid "`dllexport`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17680 ../../../AttributeReference.md:17681
msgid "`gnu::dllexport`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17689
msgid ""
"The `__declspec(dllexport)` attribute declares a variable, function, or "
"Objective-C interface to be exported from the module. It is available under "
"the `-fdeclspec` flag for compatibility with various compilers. The primary "
"use is for COFF object files which explicitly specify what interfaces are "
"available for external use. See the [dllexport][dllexport] documentation on "
"MSDN for more information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17699
msgid "dllimport"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17712 ../../../AttributeReference.md:17715
msgid "`dllimport`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17713 ../../../AttributeReference.md:17714
msgid "`gnu::dllimport`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17722
msgid ""
"The `__declspec(dllimport)` attribute declares a variable, function, or "
"Objective-C interface to be imported from an external module. It is "
"available under the `-fdeclspec` flag for compatibility with various "
"compilers. The primary use is for COFF object files which explicitly specify "
"what interfaces are imported from external modules. See the [dllimport]"
"[dllimport] documentation on MSDN for more information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17729
msgid ""
"Note that a dllimport function may still be inlined, if its definition is "
"available and it doesn't reference any non-dllimport functions or global "
"variables."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17736
msgid "ext_builtin_input"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17750
msgid "`vk::ext_builtin_input`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17759
msgid ""
"Vulkan shaders have `Input` builtins. Those variables are externally "
"initialized by the driver/pipeline, but each copy is private to the current "
"lane."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17763
msgid ""
"Those builtins can be declared using the `[[vk::ext_builtin_input]]` "
"attribute like follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17771
msgid ""
"This variable will be lowered into a module-level variable, with the `Input` "
"storage class, and the `BuiltIn 26` decoration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17774 ../../../AttributeReference.md:17816
msgid ""
"The full documentation for this inline SPIR-V attribute can be found here: "
"<https://github.com/microsoft/hlsl-specs/blob/main/proposals/0011-inline-"
"spirv.md>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17778
msgid "ext_builtin_output"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17792
msgid "`vk::ext_builtin_output`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17801
msgid ""
"Vulkan shaders have `Output` builtins. Those variables are externally "
"visible to the driver/pipeline, but each copy is private to the current lane."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17805
msgid ""
"Those builtins can be declared using the `[[vk::ext_builtin_output]]` "
"attribute like follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17813
msgid ""
"This variable will be lowered into a module-level variable, with the "
"`Output` storage class, and the `BuiltIn 0` decoration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17820
msgid "groupshared"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17837
msgid "`groupshared`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17843
msgid ""
"HLSL enables threads of a compute shader to exchange values via shared "
"memory. HLSL provides barrier primitives such as "
"GroupMemoryBarrierWithGroupSync, and so on to ensure the correct ordering of "
"reads and writes to shared memory in the shader and to avoid data races. "
"Here's an example to declare a groupshared variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17853
msgid ""
"The full documentation is available here: <https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/"
"windows/win32/direct3dhlsl/dx-graphics-hlsl-variable-syntax#group-shared>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17856
msgid "init_priority"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17869
msgid "`init_priority`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17870
msgid "`gnu::init_priority`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17879
msgid ""
"In C++, the order in which global variables are initialized across "
"translation units is unspecified, unlike the ordering within a single "
"translation unit. The `init_priority` attribute allows you to specify a "
"relative ordering for the initialization of objects declared at namespace "
"scope in C++ within a single linked image on supported platforms. The "
"priority is given as an integer constant expression between 101 and 65535 "
"(inclusive). Priorities outside of that range are reserved for use by the "
"implementation. A lower value indicates a higher priority of initialization. "
"Note that only the relative ordering of values is important. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17895
msgid ""
"`Obj2` will be initialized *before* `Obj1` despite the usual order of "
"initialization being the opposite."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17898
msgid ""
"Note that this attribute does not control the initialization order of "
"objects across final linked image boundaries like shared objects and "
"executables."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17901
msgid ""
"On Windows, `init_seg(compiler)` is represented with a priority of 200 and "
"`init_seg(library)` is represented with a priority of 400. `init_seg(user)` "
"uses the default 65535 priority."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17905
msgid ""
"On MachO platforms, this attribute also does not control the order of "
"initialization across translation units, where it only affects the order "
"within a single TU."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17908
msgid ""
"This attribute is only supported for C++ and Objective-C++ and is ignored in "
"other language modes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17912
msgid "init_seg"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17930
msgid "`init_seg`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17935
msgid ""
"The attribute applied by `pragma init_seg()` controls the section into which "
"global initialization function pointers are emitted. It is only available "
"with `-fms-extensions`. Typically, this function pointer is emitted into "
"`.CRT$XCU` on Windows. The user can change the order of initialization by "
"using a different section name with the same `.CRT$XC` prefix and a suffix "
"that sorts lexicographically before or after the standard `.CRT$XCU` "
"sections. See the [init_seg][init_seg] documentation on MSDN for more "
"information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17947
msgid "leaf"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17960
msgid "`leaf`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17961 ../../../AttributeReference.md:17962
msgid "`gnu::leaf`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17970
msgid ""
"The `leaf` attribute is used as a compiler hint to improve dataflow analysis "
"in library functions. Functions marked with the `leaf` attribute are not "
"allowed to jump back into the caller's translation unit, whether through "
"invoking a callback function, an external function call, use of `longjmp`, "
"or other means. Therefore, they cannot use or modify any data that does not "
"escape the caller function's compilation unit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17977
msgid ""
"For more information see the [GCC common attributes documentation](https://"
"gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Common-Function-Attributes.html)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17981
msgid "loader_uninitialized"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17994
msgid "`loader_uninitialized`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:17995 ../../../AttributeReference.md:17996
msgid "`clang::loader_uninitialized`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18004
msgid ""
"The `loader_uninitialized` attribute can be placed on global variables to "
"indicate that the variable does not need to be zero initialized by the "
"loader. On most targets, zero-initialization does not incur any additional "
"cost. For example, most general purpose operating systems deliberately "
"ensure that all memory is properly initialized in order to avoid leaking "
"privileged information from the kernel or other programs. However, some "
"targets do not make this guarantee, and on these targets, avoiding an "
"unnecessary zero-initialization can have a significant impact on load times "
"and/or code size."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18014
msgid ""
"A declaration with this attribute is a non-tentative definition just as if "
"it provided an initializer. Variables with this attribute are considered to "
"be uninitialized in the same sense as a local variable, and the programs "
"must write to them before reading from them. If the variable's type is a C++ "
"class type with a non-trivial default constructor, or an array thereof, this "
"attribute only suppresses the static zero-initialization of the variable, "
"not the dynamic initialization provided by executing the default constructor."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18023
msgid "location"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18037
msgid "`vk::location`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18046
msgid ""
"Attribute used for specifying the location number for the stage input/output "
"variables. Allowed on function parameters, function returns, and struct "
"fields. This parameter has no effect when used outside of an entrypoint "
"parameter/parameter field/return value."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18051
msgid "This attribute maps to the `Location` SPIR-V decoration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18054
msgid "maybe_undef"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18067
msgid "`maybe_undef`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18068 ../../../AttributeReference.md:18069
msgid "`clang::maybe_undef`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18077
msgid ""
"The `maybe_undef` attribute can be placed on a function parameter. It "
"indicates that the parameter is allowed to use undef values. It informs the "
"compiler to insert a freeze LLVM IR instruction on the function parameter. "
"Please note that this is an attribute that is used as an internal "
"implementation detail and not intended to be used by external users."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18083
msgid ""
"In languages HIP, CUDA etc., some functions have multi-threaded semantics "
"and it is enough for only one or some threads to provide defined arguments. "
"Depending on semantics, undef arguments in some threads don't produce "
"undefined results in the function call. Since, these functions accept "
"undefined arguments, `maybe_undef` attribute can be placed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18096
msgid "maybe_unused, unused"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18109
msgid "`unused`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18110
msgid "`maybe_unused` <br/> `gnu::unused`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18111
msgid "`gnu::unused` <br/> `maybe_unused`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18119
msgid ""
"When passing the `-Wunused` flag to Clang, entities that are unused by the "
"program may be diagnosed. The `[[maybe_unused]]` (or "
"`__attribute__((unused))`) attribute can be used to silence such diagnostics "
"when the entity cannot be removed. For instance, a local variable may exist "
"solely for use in an `assert()` statement, which makes the local variable "
"unused when `NDEBUG` is defined."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18126
msgid ""
"The attribute may be applied to the declaration of a class, a typedef, a "
"variable, a function or method, a function parameter, an enumeration, an "
"enumerator, a non-static data member, or a label."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18141
msgid "model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18154
msgid "`model`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18155 ../../../AttributeReference.md:18156
msgid "`gnu::model`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18164
msgid ""
"The `model` attribute allows overriding the translation unit's code model "
"(specified by `-mcmodel`) for a specific global variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18167
msgid "On LoongArch, allowed values are \"normal\", \"medium\", \"extreme\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18169
msgid ""
"On x86-64, allowed values are `\"small\"` and `\"large\"`. `\"small\"` is "
"roughly equivalent to `-mcmodel=small`, meaning the global is considered "
"\"small\" placed closer to the `.text` section relative to \"large\" "
"globals, and to prefer using 32-bit relocations to access the global. "
"`\"large\"` is roughly equivalent to `-mcmodel=large`, meaning the global is "
"considered \"large\" and placed further from the `.text` section relative to "
"\"small\" globals, and 64-bit relocations must be used to access the global."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18178
msgid "no_destroy"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18191
msgid "`no_destroy`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18192
msgid "`clang::no_destroy`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18201
msgid ""
"The `no_destroy` attribute specifies that a variable with static or thread "
"storage duration shouldn't have its exit-time destructor run. Annotating "
"every static and thread duration variable with this attribute is equivalent "
"to invoking clang with `-fno-c++-static-destructors`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18206
msgid ""
"If a variable is declared with this attribute, clang doesn't access check or "
"generate the type's destructor. If you have a type that you only want to be "
"annotated with `no_destroy`, you can therefore declare the destructor "
"private:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18220
msgid ""
"Note that destructors are still required for subobjects of aggregates "
"annotated with this attribute. This is because previously constructed "
"subobjects need to be destroyed if an exception gets thrown before the "
"initialization of the complete object is complete. For instance:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18236
msgid ""
"Here, if the construction of `array[9]` fails with an exception, "
"`array[0..8]` will be destroyed, so the element's destructor needs to be "
"accessible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18240
msgid "nodebug"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18253
msgid "`nodebug`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18254 ../../../AttributeReference.md:18255
msgid "`gnu::nodebug`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18263
msgid ""
"The `nodebug` attribute allows you to suppress debugging information for a "
"function or method, for a variable that is not a parameter or a non-static "
"data member, or for a typedef or using declaration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18268
msgid "noescape"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18281
msgid "`noescape`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18282 ../../../AttributeReference.md:18283
msgid "`clang::noescape`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18291
msgid ""
"`noescape` placed on a function parameter of a pointer type is used to "
"inform the compiler that the pointer cannot escape: that is, no reference to "
"the object the pointer points to that is derived from the parameter value "
"will survive after the function returns. Users are responsible for making "
"sure parameters annotated with `noescape` do not actually escape. The "
"optimizer may make assumptions based on the fact that it knows that a call "
"to the function does not escape a certain parameter, so incorrectly "
"annotating a parameter with `noescape` leads to undefined behavior. The "
"callee is also not allowed to deallocate memory through a `noescape` "
"parameter: the optimizer does not make assumptions based on this information "
"at the moment, but may do so in the future. Some cases of invalid uses of "
"`noescape` can be found with {ref}`-Wlifetime-safety-noescape <Wlifetime-"
"safety-noescape>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18322
msgid ""
"Since `noescape` is a parameter attribute and not a type attribute, it only "
"applies to the outermost pointer level, regardless of where in the parameter "
"declaration you place it:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18335
msgid ""
"Additionally, when the parameter is a {doc}`block pointer "
"<BlockLanguageSpec>`, the same restriction applies to copies of the block. "
"For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18353
msgid ""
"The function *is* allowed to leak information about the memory address of "
"the pointer, but not any provenance of the allocation:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18384
msgid "nosvm"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18397
msgid "`nosvm`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18407
msgid ""
"OpenCL 2.0 supports the optional `__attribute__((nosvm))` qualifier for "
"pointer variable. It informs the compiler that the pointer does not refer to "
"a shared virtual memory region. See OpenCL v2.0 s6.7.2 for details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18411
msgid ""
"Since it is not widely used and has been removed from OpenCL 2.1, it is "
"ignored by Clang."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18415
msgid "objc_externally_retained"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18428
msgid "`objc_externally_retained`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18429 ../../../AttributeReference.md:18430
msgid "`clang::objc_externally_retained`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18438
msgid ""
"The `objc_externally_retained` attribute can be applied to strong local "
"variables, functions, methods, or blocks to opt into {ref}`externally-"
"retained semantics <arc.misc.externally_retained>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18442
msgid ""
"When applied to the definition of a function, method, or block, every "
"parameter of the function with implicit strong retainable object pointer "
"type is considered externally-retained, and becomes `const`. By explicitly "
"annotating a parameter with `__strong`, you can opt back into the default "
"non-externally-retained behavior for that parameter. For instance, "
"`first_param` is externally-retained below, but not `second_param`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18456
msgid ""
"Likewise, when applied to a strong local variable, that variable becomes "
"`const` and is considered externally-retained."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18459
msgid "When compiled without `-fobjc-arc`, this attribute is ignored."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18462
msgid "pass_object_size, pass_dynamic_object_size"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18475
msgid "`pass_object_size` <br/> `pass_dynamic_object_size`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18476 ../../../AttributeReference.md:18477
msgid "`clang::pass_object_size` <br/> `clang::pass_dynamic_object_size`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18486
msgid ""
"The mangling of functions with parameters that are annotated with "
"`pass_object_size` is subject to change. You can get around this by using "
"`__asm__(\"foo\")` to explicitly name your functions, thus preserving your "
"ABI; also, non-overloadable C functions with `pass_object_size` are not "
"mangled."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18493
msgid ""
"The `pass_object_size(Type)` attribute can be placed on function parameters "
"to instruct clang to call `__builtin_object_size(param, Type)` at each "
"callsite of said function, and implicitly pass the result of this call in as "
"an invisible argument of type `size_t` directly after the parameter "
"annotated with `pass_object_size`. Clang will also replace any calls to "
"`__builtin_object_size(param, Type)` in the function by said implicit "
"parameter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18521
msgid ""
"If successfully evaluating `__builtin_object_size(param, Type)` at the "
"callsite is not possible, then the \"failed\" value is passed in. So, using "
"the definition of `bzero1` from above, the following code would exit cleanly:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18533
msgid ""
"`pass_object_size` plays a part in overload resolution. If two overload "
"candidates are otherwise equally good, then the overload with one or more "
"parameters with `pass_object_size` is preferred. This implies that the "
"choice between two identical overloads both with `pass_object_size` on one "
"or more parameters will always be ambiguous; for this reason, having two "
"such overloads is illegal. For example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18567
msgid ""
"Currently, `pass_object_size` is a bit restricted in terms of its usage:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18569
msgid "Only one use of `pass_object_size` is allowed per parameter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18570
msgid ""
"It is an error to take the address of a function with `pass_object_size` on "
"any of its parameters. If you wish to do this, you can create an overload "
"without `pass_object_size` on any parameters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18573
msgid ""
"It is an error to apply the `pass_object_size` attribute to parameters that "
"are not pointers. Additionally, any parameter that `pass_object_size` is "
"applied to must be marked `const` at its function's definition."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18577
msgid ""
"Clang also supports the `pass_dynamic_object_size` attribute, which behaves "
"identically to `pass_object_size`, but evaluates a call to "
"`__builtin_dynamic_object_size` at the callee instead of "
"`__builtin_object_size`. `__builtin_dynamic_object_size` provides some extra "
"runtime checks when the object size can't be determined at compile-time. You "
"can read more about `__builtin_dynamic_object_size` in {ref}`Evaluating "
"Object Size <langext-evaluating-object-size>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18586
msgid "push_constant"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18600
msgid "`vk::push_constant`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18609
msgid "Vulkan shaders have `PushConstants`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18611
msgid ""
"The `[[vk::push_constant]]` attribute allows you to declare this global "
"variable as a push constant when targeting Vulkan. This attribute is ignored "
"otherwise."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18615
msgid ""
"This attribute must be applied to the variable, not underlying type. The "
"variable type must be a struct, per the requirements of Vulkan, \"there must "
"be no more than one push constant block statically used per shader entry "
"point.\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18621
msgid "require_constant_initialization, constinit (C++20)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18634
msgid "`require_constant_initialization`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18635
msgid "`clang::require_constant_initialization`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18638
msgid "`constinit`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18644
msgid ""
"This attribute specifies that the variable to which it is attached is "
"intended to have a [*constant initializer*](http://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/"
"language/constant_initialization) according to the rules of "
"[basic.start.static]. The variable is required to have static or thread "
"storage duration. If the initialization of the variable is not a constant "
"initializer an error will be produced. This attribute may only be used in C+"
"+; the `constinit` spelling is only accepted in C++20 onwards."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18652
msgid ""
"Note that in C++03 strict constant expression checking is not done. Instead "
"the attribute reports if Clang can emit the variable as a constant, even if "
"it's not technically a *constant initializer*. This behavior is non-portable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18656
msgid ""
"Static storage duration variables with constant initializers avoid hard-to-"
"find bugs caused by the indeterminate order of dynamic initialization. They "
"can also be safely used during dynamic initialization across translation "
"units."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18660
msgid ""
"This attribute acts as a compile time assertion that the requirements for "
"constant initialization have been met. Since these requirements change "
"between dialects and have subtle pitfalls it's important to fail fast "
"instead of silently falling back on dynamic initialization."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18665
msgid ""
"The first use of the attribute on a variable must be part of, or precede, "
"the initializing declaration of the variable. C++20 requires the `constinit` "
"spelling of the attribute to be present on the initializing declaration if "
"it is used anywhere. The other spellings can be specified on a forward "
"declaration and omitted on a later initializing declaration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18684
msgid "row_major, column_major"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18701
msgid "`column_major` <br/> `row_major`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18707
msgid ""
"The `row_major` and `column_major` keywords specify the memory layout of an "
"HLSL matrix type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18710
msgid "`row_major`: Matrices are stored in memory row-by-row."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18711
msgid ""
"`column_major`: Matrices are stored in memory column-by-column (default)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18720
msgid "section, __declspec(allocate)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18733
msgid "`section`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18734 ../../../AttributeReference.md:18735
msgid "`gnu::section`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18736
msgid "`allocate`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18743
msgid ""
"The `section` attribute allows you to specify a specific section a global "
"variable or function should be in after translation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18747
msgid "standalone_debug"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18760
msgid "`standalone_debug`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18761
msgid "`clang::standalone_debug`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18770
msgid ""
"The `standalone_debug` attribute causes debug info to be emitted for a "
"record type regardless of the debug info optimizations that are enabled with "
"-fno-standalone-debug. This attribute only has an effect when debug info "
"optimizations are enabled (e.g. with -fno-standalone-debug), and is C++-only."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18776
msgid "swift_async_context"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18789
msgid "`swift_async_context`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18790 ../../../AttributeReference.md:18791
msgid "`clang::swift_async_context`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18799
msgid ""
"The `swift_async_context` attribute marks a parameter of a `swiftasynccall` "
"function as having the special asynchronous context-parameter ABI treatment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18802
msgid ""
"If the function is not `swiftasynccall`, this attribute only generates "
"extended frame information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18805 ../../../AttributeReference.md:18842
msgid "A context parameter must have pointer or reference type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18808
msgid "swift_context"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18821
msgid "`swift_context`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18822 ../../../AttributeReference.md:18823
msgid "`clang::swift_context`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18831
msgid ""
"The `swift_context` attribute marks a parameter of a `swiftcall` or "
"`swiftasynccall` function as having the special context-parameter ABI "
"treatment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18835
msgid ""
"This treatment generally passes the context value in a special register "
"which is normally callee-preserved."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18838
msgid ""
"A `swift_context` parameter must either be the last parameter or must be "
"followed by a `swift_error_result` parameter (which itself must always be "
"the last parameter)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18845
msgid "swift_error_result"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18858
msgid "`swift_error_result`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18859 ../../../AttributeReference.md:18860
msgid "`clang::swift_error_result`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18868
msgid ""
"The `swift_error_result` attribute marks a parameter of a `swiftcall` "
"function as having the special error-result ABI treatment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18871
msgid ""
"This treatment generally passes the underlying error value in and out of the "
"function through a special register which is normally callee-preserved. This "
"is modeled in C by pretending that the register is addressable memory:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18875
msgid ""
"The caller appears to pass the address of a variable of pointer type. The "
"current value of this variable is copied into the register before the call; "
"if the call returns normally, the value is copied back into the variable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18879
msgid ""
"The callee appears to receive the address of a variable. This address is "
"actually a hidden location in its own stack, initialized with the value of "
"the register upon entry. When the function returns normally, the value in "
"that hidden location is written back to the register."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18884
msgid ""
"A `swift_error_result` parameter must be the last parameter, and it must be "
"preceded by a `swift_context` parameter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18887
msgid ""
"A `swift_error_result` parameter must have type `T**` or `T*&` for some type "
"T. Note that no qualifiers are permitted on the intermediate level."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18890
msgid ""
"It is undefined behavior if the caller does not pass a pointer or reference "
"to a valid object."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18893
msgid ""
"The standard convention is that the error value itself (that is, the value "
"stored in the apparent argument) will be null upon function entry, but this "
"is not enforced by the ABI."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18898
msgid "swift_indirect_result"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18911
msgid "`swift_indirect_result`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18912 ../../../AttributeReference.md:18913
msgid "`clang::swift_indirect_result`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18921
msgid ""
"The `swift_indirect_result` attribute marks a parameter of a `swiftcall` or "
"`swiftasynccall` function as having the special indirect-result ABI "
"treatment."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18925
msgid ""
"This treatment gives the parameter the target's normal indirect-result ABI "
"treatment, which may involve passing it differently from an ordinary "
"parameter. However, only the first indirect result will receive this "
"treatment. Furthermore, low-level lowering may decide that a direct result "
"must be returned indirectly; if so, this will take priority over the "
"`swift_indirect_result` parameters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18932
msgid ""
"A `swift_indirect_result` parameter must either be the first parameter or "
"follow another `swift_indirect_result` parameter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18935
msgid ""
"A `swift_indirect_result` parameter must have type `T*` or `T&` for some "
"object type `T`. If `T` is a complete type at the point of definition of a "
"function, it is undefined behavior if the argument value does not point to "
"storage of adequate size and alignment for a value of type `T`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18941
msgid ""
"Making indirect results explicit in the signature allows C functions to "
"directly construct objects into them without relying on language "
"optimizations like C++'s named return value optimization (NRVO)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18946
msgid "swiftasynccall"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18959
msgid "`swiftasynccall`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18960 ../../../AttributeReference.md:18961
msgid "`clang::swiftasynccall`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18969
msgid ""
"The `swiftasynccall` attribute indicates that a function is compatible with "
"the low-level conventions of Swift async functions, provided it declares the "
"right formal arguments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18973
msgid ""
"In most respects, this is similar to the `swiftcall` attribute, except for "
"the following:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18976
msgid ""
"A parameter may be marked `swift_async_context`, `swift_context` or "
"`swift_indirect_result` (with the same restrictions on parameter ordering as "
"`swiftcall`) but the parameter attribute `swift_error_result` is not "
"permitted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18980
msgid "A `swiftasynccall` function must have return type `void`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18981
msgid ""
"Within a `swiftasynccall` function, a call to a `swiftasynccall` function "
"that is the immediate operand of a `return` statement is guaranteed to be "
"performed as a tail call. This syntax is allowed even in C as an extension "
"(a call to a void-returning function cannot be a return operand in standard "
"C). If something in the calling function would semantically be performed "
"after a guaranteed tail call, such as the non-trivial destruction of a local "
"variable or temporary, then the program is ill-formed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18990
msgid ""
"Query for this attribute with `__has_attribute(swiftasynccall)`. Query if "
"the target supports the calling convention with "
"`__has_extension(swiftasynccc)`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:18994
msgid ""
"Since this attribute follows the Swift async calling convention, it is "
"considered ABI-unstable except on targets where the Swift project has "
"declared ABI stability. Users are responsible for ensuring that calls and "
"definitions of functions with this attribute are compiled with compatible "
"compilers. Note that different operating systems on the same architecture "
"may use different ABIs and therefore may have different standards for ABI "
"stability."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19003
msgid "swiftcall"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19016
msgid "`swiftcall`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19017 ../../../AttributeReference.md:19018
msgid "`clang::swiftcall`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19026
msgid ""
"The `swiftcall` attribute indicates that a function should be called using "
"the Swift calling convention for a function or function pointer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19029
msgid ""
"The lowering for the Swift calling convention, as described by the Swift ABI "
"documentation, occurs in multiple phases. The first, \"high-level\" phase "
"breaks down the formal parameters and results into innately direct and "
"indirect components, adds implicit parameters for the generic signature, and "
"assigns the context and error ABI treatments to parameters where applicable. "
"The second phase breaks down the direct parameters and results from the "
"first phase and assigns them to registers or the stack. The `swiftcall` "
"convention only handles this second phase of lowering; the C function type "
"must accurately reflect the results of the first phase, as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19040
msgid ""
"Results classified as indirect by high-level lowering should be represented "
"as parameters with the `swift_indirect_result` attribute."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19043
msgid ""
"Results classified as direct by high-level lowering should be represented as "
"follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19046
msgid "First, remove any empty direct results."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19047
msgid "If there are no direct results, the C result type should be `void`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19048
msgid ""
"If there is one direct result, the C result type should be a type with the "
"exact layout of that result type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19050
msgid ""
"If there are a multiple direct results, the C result type should be a struct "
"type with the exact layout of a tuple of those results."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19053
msgid ""
"Parameters classified as indirect by high-level lowering should be "
"represented as parameters of pointer type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19056
msgid ""
"Parameters classified as direct by high-level lowering should be omitted if "
"they are empty types; otherwise, they should be represented as a parameter "
"type with a layout exactly matching the layout of the Swift parameter type."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19061
msgid ""
"The context parameter, if present, should be represented as a trailing "
"parameter with the `swift_context` attribute."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19064
msgid ""
"The error result parameter, if present, should be represented as a trailing "
"parameter (always following a context parameter) with the "
"`swift_error_result` attribute."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19068
msgid ""
"`swiftcall` does not support variadic arguments or unprototyped functions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19070
msgid ""
"The parameter ABI treatment attributes are aspects of the function type. A "
"function type which applies an ABI treatment attribute to a parameter is a "
"different type from an otherwise-identical function type that does not. A "
"single parameter may not have multiple ABI treatment attributes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19076
msgid ""
"Support for this feature is target-dependent, although it should be "
"supported on every target that Swift supports. Query for this attribute with "
"`__has_attribute(swiftcall)`. Query if the target supports the calling "
"convention with `__has_extension(swiftcc)`. This implies support for the "
"`swift_context`, `swift_error_result`, and `swift_indirect_result` "
"attributes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19083
msgid ""
"Since this attribute follows the Swift calling convention, it is considered "
"ABI-unstable except on targets where the Swift project has declared ABI "
"stability. Users are responsible for ensuring that calls and definitions of "
"functions with this attribute are compiled with compatible compilers. Note "
"that different operating systems on the same architecture may use different "
"ABIs and therefore may have different standards for ABI stability."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19092
msgid "thread"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19108
msgid "`thread`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19115
msgid ""
"The `__declspec(thread)` attribute declares a variable with thread local "
"storage. It is available under the `-fms-extensions` flag for MSVC "
"compatibility. See the documentation for [`__declspec(thread)`]"
"[__declspec(thread)] on MSDN."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19119
msgid ""
"In Clang, `__declspec(thread)` is generally equivalent in functionality to "
"the GNU `__thread` keyword. The variable must not have a destructor and must "
"have a constant initializer, if any. The attribute only applies to variables "
"declared with static storage duration, such as globals, class static data "
"members, and static locals."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19128
msgid "tls_model"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19141
msgid "`tls_model`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19142 ../../../AttributeReference.md:19143
msgid "`gnu::tls_model`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19151
msgid ""
"The `tls_model` attribute allows you to specify which thread-local storage "
"model to use. It accepts the following strings:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19154
msgid "global-dynamic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19155
msgid "local-dynamic"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19156
msgid "initial-exec"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19157
msgid "local-exec"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19159
msgid "TLS models are mutually exclusive."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19162
msgid "uninitialized"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19175
msgid "`uninitialized`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19176
msgid "`clang::uninitialized`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AttributeReference.md:19185
msgid ""
"The command-line parameter `-ftrivial-auto-var-init=*` can be used to "
"initialize trivial automatic stack variables. By default, trivial automatic "
"stack variables are uninitialized. This attribute is used to override the "
"command-line parameter, forcing variables to remain uninitialized. It has no "
"semantic meaning in that using uninitialized values is undefined behavior, "
"it rather documents the programmer's intent."
msgstr ""
